1
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:02,335
Tomorrow is Halloween,

2
00:00:02,335 --> 00:00:04,694
and kids go door to door shouting trick or

3
00:00:04,694 --> 00:00:06,940
treat: give us candy or we play a prank!

4
00:00:07,140 --> 00:00:11,749
The cocoa in those chocolates now costs less than half its peak,

5
00:00:11,749 --> 00:00:14,270
yet chocolate prices haven't moved.

6
00:00:15,070 --> 00:00:18,146
Meanwhile West African cocoa farmers saw their

7
00:00:18,146 --> 00:00:19,884
pay cut by more than half,

8
00:00:19,884 --> 00:00:22,960
and Hershey sold 10% less candy yet kept more.

9
00:00:23,160 --> 00:00:25,664
Today we follow one bag of chocolate from the

10
00:00:25,664 --> 00:00:28,169
weather on the other side of the world to the

11
00:00:28,169 --> 00:00:29,560
price of Pepero in Korea.

12
00:00:30,360 --> 00:00:32,408
The answer hides behind the price tag,

13
00:00:32,408 --> 00:00:35,210
and at the end I'll give you three numbers to watch.

14
00:00:36,910 --> 00:00:37,393
Part one,

15
00:00:37,393 --> 00:00:39,810
the big picture first: these days the weather

16
00:00:39,810 --> 00:00:41,850
sets the price of what's on our table.

17
00:00:42,650 --> 00:00:45,625
In 2024 the weather didn't just hit cocoa:

18
00:00:45,625 --> 00:00:48,316
orange juice hit a record at year-end,

19
00:00:48,316 --> 00:00:51,432
and coffee touched 4 dollars a pound for the

20
00:00:51,432 --> 00:00:53,840
first time after Brazil's drought.

21
00:00:54,040 --> 00:00:57,002
In Europe olive oil followed: after two bad

22
00:00:57,002 --> 00:00:58,793
Spanish harvests in a row,

23
00:00:58,793 --> 00:01:01,410
farm-gate prices jumped 68% in a year.

24
00:01:02,210 --> 00:01:05,073
But when the weather eases and raw prices fall,

25
00:01:05,073 --> 00:01:06,900
shelf prices barely come down.

26
00:01:07,090 --> 00:01:09,327
Spanish olive oil at the farm gate is at a

27
00:01:09,327 --> 00:01:10,658
five-year low this month,

28
00:01:10,658 --> 00:01:13,640
yet supermarket bottles still cost over 4 euros a litre.

29
00:01:14,450 --> 00:01:19,031
Economists call it rockets and feathers: up like a rocket,

30
00:01:19,031 --> 00:01:20,610
down like a feather.

31
00:01:20,610 --> 00:01:24,085
ECB researchers estimate warming alone could

32
00:01:24,085 --> 00:01:28,350
add up to 3.2 points a year to food inflation by 2035.

33
00:01:28,540 --> 00:01:31,570
The Halloween chocolate story is one scene in that bigger trend.

34
00:01:33,270 --> 00:01:35,380
Part two: what Americans spend on Halloween.

35
00:01:36,180 --> 00:01:38,590
The National Retail Federation expects

36
00:01:38,590 --> 00:01:42,523
Americans to spend a record 13.5 billion dollars on Halloween,

37
00:01:42,523 --> 00:01:45,250
topping last year's record of 13.1 billion.

38
00:01:45,450 --> 00:01:47,534
That's 115 dollars per person,

39
00:01:47,534 --> 00:01:50,590
and nearly three in four people taking part.

40
00:01:51,390 --> 00:01:54,162
Where does it go? 4.4 billion on costumes,

41
00:01:54,162 --> 00:01:55,944
4.3 billion on decorations,

42
00:01:55,944 --> 00:01:57,330
4.1 billion on candy.

43
00:01:57,530 --> 00:01:59,187
Candy is the symbol of Halloween,

44
00:01:59,187 --> 00:02:01,597
yet more money goes to costumes and decorations.

45
00:02:01,597 --> 00:02:02,200
First twist.

46
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:06,563
Split the 4.4 billion: adults 2 billion,

47
00:02:06,563 --> 00:02:10,036
kids 1.5 billion, and pets 920 million.

48
00:02:10,036 --> 00:02:13,510
Top pet costume: pumpkin, then hot dog.

49
00:02:13,710 --> 00:02:16,463
Whether the dogs wanted to be hot dogs,

50
00:02:16,463 --> 00:02:17,380
nobody asked.

51
00:02:18,180 --> 00:02:20,288
But candy moves the other way.

52
00:02:20,288 --> 00:02:22,114
NielsenIQ data showed U.S.

53
00:02:22,114 --> 00:02:26,540
candy sales down 11.7% from a year earlier as of mid-September.

54
00:02:26,740 --> 00:02:27,652
Less candy sold,

55
00:02:27,652 --> 00:02:29,647
more money made by the candy maker.

56
00:02:29,647 --> 00:02:31,300
That's today's real question.

57
00:02:33,010 --> 00:02:33,751
Part three,

58
00:02:33,751 --> 00:02:38,195
winter 2024: drought brought by El Niño hit Ivory Coast and Ghana,

59
00:02:38,195 --> 00:02:42,100
which usually grow nearly two-thirds of the world's cocoa.

60
00:02:42,300 --> 00:02:43,505
With disease spreading,

61
00:02:43,505 --> 00:02:45,862
cocoa that sold for about 2,500 dollars a ton

62
00:02:45,862 --> 00:02:47,800
rose more than fivefold in two years.

63
00:02:48,600 --> 00:02:50,277
On December 18, 2024,

64
00:02:50,277 --> 00:02:53,712
a ton of cocoa hit 12,931 dollars intraday,

65
00:02:53,712 --> 00:02:55,070
an all-time high.

66
00:02:55,270 --> 00:02:57,487
A year later it was in the 6,000s,

67
00:02:57,487 --> 00:03:00,095
and early this month it's in the 5,000s,

68
00:03:00,095 --> 00:03:01,660
less than half the peak.

69
00:03:01,860 --> 00:03:04,985
Supply came back: by early October Ivory Coast

70
00:03:04,985 --> 00:03:07,975
shipments were up 19.8% from a year earlier,

71
00:03:07,975 --> 00:03:10,760
and exchange stocks hit a 2.25-year high.

72
00:03:11,550 --> 00:03:13,184
When prices turned,

73
00:03:13,184 --> 00:03:17,227
farmers were cut first: Ivory Coast's set cocoa

74
00:03:17,227 --> 00:03:22,130
price fell from 2,800 to 1,200 francs a kilo this spring.

75
00:03:22,340 --> 00:03:24,487
Farmers' income more than halved,

76
00:03:24,487 --> 00:03:27,480
while the price of a chocolate bag stayed put.

77
00:03:28,270 --> 00:03:31,795
It's not the first time one country moved

78
00:03:31,795 --> 00:03:35,751
cocoa: in 2011 a disputed Ivory Coast election

79
00:03:35,751 --> 00:03:39,190
led to an export ban and a 32-year high.

80
00:03:39,390 --> 00:03:42,138
That time people blocked the road and talks reopened it.

81
00:03:42,138 --> 00:03:44,150
This time the weather shrank the harvest.

82
00:03:45,850 --> 00:03:46,485
Part four,

83
00:03:46,485 --> 00:03:48,835
Hershey's report card two days before

84
00:03:48,835 --> 00:03:51,884
Halloween: price hikes added about 11% to sales,

85
00:03:51,884 --> 00:03:53,980
lower volume took away about 10%.

86
00:03:54,780 --> 00:03:57,996
If a family that bought ten bags now buys nine,

87
00:03:57,996 --> 00:03:59,911
but each bag costs 11% more,

88
00:03:59,911 --> 00:04:01,690
sales stay about the same.

89
00:04:01,890 --> 00:04:06,644
Hershey's sales actually rose 3% to 3.28 billion dollars.

90
00:04:06,644 --> 00:04:08,980
Less sold, yet more revenue.

91
00:04:09,780 --> 00:04:12,908
But the money left over changed dramatically.

92
00:04:12,908 --> 00:04:15,132
Adjusted gross margin hit 42.7%,

93
00:04:15,132 --> 00:04:16,870
up 10.9 points in a year.

94
00:04:17,070 --> 00:04:22,020
A company that kept 32 dollars per 100 now keeps 43.

95
00:04:22,820 --> 00:04:25,178
In North America Confectionery,

96
00:04:25,178 --> 00:04:27,004
home of Halloween candy,

97
00:04:27,004 --> 00:04:30,503
it's even starker: about 13 points from price,

98
00:04:30,503 --> 00:04:32,100
volume down about 11.

99
00:04:32,300 --> 00:04:34,379
Fewer shoppers at the candy shelf,

100
00:04:34,379 --> 00:04:36,030
bigger numbers on the tags.

101
00:04:36,830 --> 00:04:39,927
Hershey's CEO said the results came even as

102
00:04:39,927 --> 00:04:42,016
consumers grow more cautious.

103
00:04:42,016 --> 00:04:45,257
Prices went up while wallets were tightening,

104
00:04:45,257 --> 00:04:46,770
and the company held.

105
00:04:48,470 --> 00:04:51,027
Chapter four, back to the first question.

106
00:04:51,027 --> 00:04:52,835
Cocoa fell by more than half.

107
00:04:52,835 --> 00:04:55,080
Why isn't chocolate getting cheaper?

108
00:04:55,880 --> 00:04:59,151
There's a saying: prices take the elevator up when costs rise,

109
00:04:59,151 --> 00:05:01,050
and the stairs down when costs fall.

110
00:05:01,850 --> 00:05:03,326
Three reasons. First,

111
00:05:03,326 --> 00:05:05,716
chocolate makers buy cocoa months,

112
00:05:05,716 --> 00:05:08,457
sometimes more than a year, in advance.

113
00:05:08,457 --> 00:05:11,620
Today's cheap cocoa reaches costs only later.

114
00:05:11,820 --> 00:05:14,788
Second, cost isn't just cocoa: sugar, milk,

115
00:05:14,788 --> 00:05:17,410
packaging, labor and freight move too.

116
00:05:17,610 --> 00:05:18,021
Third,

117
00:05:18,021 --> 00:05:22,340
cutting a price you've raised is the last card a company plays.

118
00:05:23,140 --> 00:05:26,345
So how does Hershey raise prices and hold on?

119
00:05:26,345 --> 00:05:29,693
The answer is in the names: Hershey's, Reese's,

120
00:05:29,693 --> 00:05:32,400
Kisses, and in the U.S., even Kit Kat.

121
00:05:32,600 --> 00:05:35,299
The names kids grab first from the Halloween bowl.

122
00:05:35,299 --> 00:05:37,944
As long as shoppers pick the name over the price,

123
00:05:37,944 --> 00:05:39,510
the company can raise prices.

124
00:05:40,310 --> 00:05:43,444
Not everyone took the same road. Mondelez,

125
00:05:43,444 --> 00:05:44,563
the Oreo maker,

126
00:05:44,563 --> 00:05:48,592
reported the same week: pricing added only 0.3 points,

127
00:05:48,592 --> 00:05:50,010
volume and mix 1.8.

128
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:52,211
One wins on price, the other on volume.

129
00:05:52,211 --> 00:05:54,840
The snack aisle is running two experiments at once.

130
00:05:55,640 --> 00:05:59,041
Shoppers fight back. In the month to October 17,

131
00:05:59,041 --> 00:05:59,820
U.S. candy,

132
00:05:59,820 --> 00:06:04,000
gum and chocolate sales were down 6.7% from a year earlier.

133
00:06:04,200 --> 00:06:05,551
In the NRF survey,

134
00:06:05,551 --> 00:06:09,830
34% said higher prices would push them to reusable items,

135
00:06:09,830 --> 00:06:11,482
31% to compare prices.

136
00:06:11,482 --> 00:06:15,160
Bringing them back is the candy makers' next job.

137
00:06:16,860 --> 00:06:18,953
Chapter six: October 31 in Korea.

138
00:06:18,953 --> 00:06:20,919
Since the 2022 Itaewon tragedy,

139
00:06:20,919 --> 00:06:24,914
Korean retailers have kept Halloween low-key for a fourth year.

140
00:06:24,914 --> 00:06:27,260
Yesterday was the fourth anniversary.

141
00:06:28,060 --> 00:06:31,970
Seoul is again managing crowds at 14 locations

142
00:06:31,970 --> 00:06:34,520
from October 23 to November 1.

143
00:06:34,520 --> 00:06:37,920
If you're celebrating, please stay safe.

144
00:06:38,720 --> 00:06:42,503
Korean convenience stores have a different autumn peak: Pepero Day,

145
00:06:42,503 --> 00:06:43,180
November 11.

146
00:06:43,380 --> 00:06:49,960
GS25's Pepero Day sales rose 39.2% in 2024 and 30.5% in 2025.

147
00:06:50,760 --> 00:06:53,930
Ivory Coast's weather reaches Korean shelves:

148
00:06:53,930 --> 00:06:57,170
Lotte Wellfood raised Ghana Mild from 2,800 to

149
00:06:57,170 --> 00:06:58,860
3,400 won last February.

150
00:06:59,060 --> 00:07:01,665
The price stayed even after cocoa fell; the

151
00:07:01,665 --> 00:07:05,300
industry said expensive contracted cocoa was still arriving.

152
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,789
Going one step deeper: the cocoa market watches

153
00:07:09,789 --> 00:07:13,350
exchange warehouse stocks to see how much beans are on hand.

154
00:07:14,140 --> 00:07:17,289
Certified cocoa in New York exchange warehouses

155
00:07:17,289 --> 00:07:19,700
passed 3.63 million bags this month,

156
00:07:19,700 --> 00:07:21,509
a two-and-a-half-year high,

157
00:07:21,509 --> 00:07:23,050
which weighs on prices.

158
00:07:23,260 --> 00:07:25,181
But a 'very strong' El Niño,

159
00:07:25,181 --> 00:07:27,240
put above 90% odds by the U.S.

160
00:07:27,240 --> 00:07:29,024
Climate Prediction Center,

161
00:07:29,024 --> 00:07:30,877
threatens the next harvest.

162
00:07:30,877 --> 00:07:33,690
Chocolate prices hang on this tug of war.

163
00:07:35,380 --> 00:07:37,130
Now the three numbers I promised.

164
00:07:37,130 --> 00:07:38,880
Watch these at the next earnings.

165
00:07:39,680 --> 00:07:42,973
One: Hershey's volume, down about 10% this time.

166
00:07:42,973 --> 00:07:45,992
The key is whether it returns near zero once

167
00:07:45,992 --> 00:07:48,050
the price hikes cycle through.

168
00:07:48,250 --> 00:07:52,380
If volume drops sharply again in the Q4 report early next year,

169
00:07:52,380 --> 00:07:54,150
pricing power is weakening.

170
00:07:54,950 --> 00:07:58,135
Two: cocoa futures, now in the 5,000s.

171
00:07:58,135 --> 00:08:01,906
Some forecast El Niño could cut Ivory Coast's

172
00:08:01,906 --> 00:08:03,750
next main crop by 17%.

173
00:08:03,940 --> 00:08:05,060
If cocoa climbs again,

174
00:08:05,060 --> 00:08:07,403
forget cheaper chocolate; if it keeps falling,

175
00:08:07,403 --> 00:08:10,000
Hershey's margins could improve for a while longer.

176
00:08:10,800 --> 00:08:12,452
Three: price or volume.

177
00:08:12,452 --> 00:08:16,260
Hershey trimmed its sales growth outlook to about 3%,

178
00:08:16,260 --> 00:08:20,570
confident in pricing but cautious about how many people buy.

179
00:08:20,770 --> 00:08:21,509
Next quarter,

180
00:08:21,509 --> 00:08:24,010
put Hershey's and Mondelez's volumes side by

181
00:08:24,010 --> 00:08:27,080
side and see whether Hershey gets out of the negative.

182
00:08:27,870 --> 00:08:31,390
No one knows this power better than Warren Buffett.

183
00:08:31,390 --> 00:08:34,703
In 1972 Berkshire Hathaway bought See's Candies,

184
00:08:34,703 --> 00:08:36,567
a California candy company,

185
00:08:36,567 --> 00:08:38,155
for 25 million dollars.

186
00:08:38,155 --> 00:08:42,020
It was earning 5 million pretax on 8 million of capital.

187
00:08:42,230 --> 00:08:43,806
In his 2007 letter,

188
00:08:43,806 --> 00:08:47,457
See's had earned 1.35 billion dollars pretax

189
00:08:47,457 --> 00:08:49,780
over the following 35 years.

190
00:08:49,980 --> 00:08:52,170
That year sales were 383 million,

191
00:08:52,170 --> 00:08:54,759
with only 40 million of capital needed.

192
00:08:54,759 --> 00:08:57,679
The secret to growing profits with almost no

193
00:08:57,679 --> 00:09:01,130
extra capital: customers stay even when prices rise.

194
00:09:01,920 --> 00:09:03,757
In one line: when costs fall,

195
00:09:03,757 --> 00:09:05,278
prices come down slowly,

196
00:09:05,278 --> 00:09:09,270
and the companies with strong names keep the profit in between.

197
00:09:09,470 --> 00:09:12,777
Weather will shake raw material prices more often from here.

198
00:09:12,777 --> 00:09:14,595
Next time you see a candy basket,

199
00:09:14,595 --> 00:09:17,020
think of the structure behind the price tag.

200
00:09:17,810 --> 00:09:20,786
Buffett calls it pricing power,

201
00:09:20,786 --> 00:09:25,970
the single most important thing in judging a business.

202
00:09:26,180 --> 00:09:29,038
I keep numbers like these in a notebook every

203
00:09:29,038 --> 00:09:30,435
day; that's ValueNote.

204
00:09:30,435 --> 00:09:33,420
I'll leave my Hershey notes in the description.

205
00:09:34,210 --> 00:09:36,152
If you want more stories behind the numbers,

206
00:09:36,152 --> 00:09:38,050
please subscribe and turn on notifications.

207
00:09:38,250 --> 00:09:40,480
For the ultimate example of pricing power,

208
00:09:40,480 --> 00:09:43,930
watch the Coca-Cola stock Warren Buffett hasn't sold in 38 years.

209
00:09:44,130 --> 00:09:46,804
Did Halloween candy feel pricier this year?

210
00:09:46,804 --> 00:09:48,980
Tell us in the comments. Thank you.
